  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14267656/assets/5253ff44a3fb04cc09568e5289dd7e73d3e286651e257cf7efe20944ac57e439.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}+1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of months $x$ at which the inflation given by this function was $3\\,%.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $f$ is measured in $%.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We have to solve $f(x)=3,$ or equivalently, $2\\sqrt{x}+1=3.$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term $2\\sqrt{x}$ by subtracting $1$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x}+1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $\\frac{1}{2}$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x})^{2} & ={1}^{2} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{1}+1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 2+1 & =3\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=1$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $2\\sqrt{x}+1=3$  is $x=1.$",
